  "question": {
    "text": "A solid sphere of uniform density and radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> applies a gravitational force of attraction equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;on a particle placed at a distance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>R</mi></math> from the centre of the sphere. A spherical cavity of the radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac bevelled=\"true\"><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> is now made in the sphere as shown in the figure. The sphere with the cavity now applies a gravitational force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;on the same particle. The ratio&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></math>&nbsp;is<br />\r<br />\r<b><u><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0037_ee4351305c8f.jpg\" style=\"width: 180px; height: 136px;\" /></u></b>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math><mrow>  <mfrac>    <mrow>      <mn>9</mn>    </mrow>    <mrow>      <mn>5</mn>      <mn>0</mn>    </mrow>  </mfrac></mrow></math>",
